# WRF-WAVE: The Weather Research and Forecasting Model with Moving Wave Capability

WRF-WAVE adds a phase-resolved, moving ocean-surface-wave capability to the
Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) Model, version 3.8.1. The moving-wave
capability is implemented in the ARW (`dyn_em`) dynamical core as a prescribed
moving lower boundary that represents propagating surface waves. This enables
high-fidelity large-eddy simulation (LES) of airflow over moving surface waves.

This work is built upon the immersed-boundary method (IBM) implementation in
this WRF codebase, which was previously developed and released by Lawrence
Livermore National Laboratory (LLNL) (see
`README_notice_for_immersed_boundary_method`).

Initial moving-wave capability, v1.0 — Developer: Peiyun Zhu

## Requirements

- Built on WRF v3.8.1; the standard WRF build prerequisites apply
  (Fortran and C compilers, MPI, NetCDF).
- **Double precision is required.** WRF must be configured with the `-r8`
  option.

## Building

```
./clean -a
./configure -r8
# Select your compiler / parallelism option from the configure menu
# (for example, an Intel dmpar build).
./compile em_les
```

The moving-wave and immersed-boundary cases are built and run through the
idealized LES case (`em_les`). The wave initialization lives in
`dyn_em/module_initialize_les.F`, and the immersed-boundary routines are in
`dyn_em/module_ibm.F`.

## Running

The moving-wave configuration (wave amplitude, frequency, wavenumber, the
`move`/`test_wave` switches, etc.) is set directly in the source code; there
is no namelist interface for these wave parameters.

## Citation

If you use this code, please cite the following publications:

- Zhu, P., Li, T., Mirocha, J. D., Arthur, R. S., Wu, Z., & Fringer, O. B.
  (2023). A moving-wave implementation in WRF to study the impact of surface
  water waves on the atmospheric boundary layer. *Monthly Weather Review*,
  151(11), 2883-2903.
- Li, T., Zhu, P., Mirocha, J. D., & Fringer, O. B. (2025). A large-eddy
  simulation study in WRF on wind over broadband waves of different directions
  and spreading widths. *Monthly Weather Review*, 153(6), 1045-1063.

